  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how fluorinated materials are used in battery electrolytes and what benefits they provide. On the left, the “Applications” side groups several electrolyte-related forms: solid inorganic electrolytes, solid polymer electrolytes, and solid hybrid electrolytes, along with fluorinated electrolyte solvents, additives, and fluorinated electrode compounds. The drawings suggest that fluorination can be incorporated across both liquid and solid battery components rather than in just one specific material type.\n\nOn the right, the “Performance” side highlights the main advantages associated with these fluorinated systems. A prominent theme is formation of an F-rich interphase, which appears linked to lower resistance and better ion transport. The figure also emphasizes prolonged cycle life, showing one trend remaining stable while another declines, implying improved durability. Safety is another key point, illustrated by a non-flammable symbol. Additional benefits include enhanced ion migration, dendrite-free deposition or growth, and overall good chemical stability, reinforced by a simple carbon–fluorine motif.\n\nTaken together, the image presents fluorination as a broad design strategy for battery materials that improves interfacial chemistry, transport, safety, and long-term electrochemical stability.",
